WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.000
When most people think about The Sopranos,
they think about that ambiguous ending.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.580
People are still discussing that final shot
to this day.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.520
But over a decade later, we think finally
understand the ending of The Sopranos.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.200
Probably.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:11.840
Hopefully.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:12.520
Maybe?

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:13.640
Well, let's break it all down.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.780
One of the most important relationships in
The Sopranos if not the most important relationship

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.520
is between mob boss Tony Soprano and his therapist,
Dr. Jennifer Melfi.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.560
For years, the two danced around each other
in Melfi's office, as the therapist was often

00:00:26.569 --> 00:00:29.710
entranced and sometimes repulsed by Tony and
his gangster ways.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.530
Making things even more complicated, Tony
was sometimes in love and sometimes sick of

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:34.140
his therapist.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.480
Through it all, the personal and professional
connection very nearly ended on more than

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.660
one occasion, but the two always seemed to
find their way back to each other.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.280
Then, in the penultimate episode of the series,
Melfi comes to a realization after a colleague

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.520
encourages her to read up on how Tony, as
a sociopath, might simply be taking advantage

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.020
of her.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.180
Convinced that he's been using her, Melfi
cuts Tony off during a tense final session.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.499
After that, Tony doesn't attempt to call or
contact her, and as far as we know, he considers

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.640
their relationship over, just as she does.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.220
Remember this it'll be important later.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.280
Loose ends and uncertain fates are a hallmark
of The Sopranos' final season, and nowhere

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.570
is that more devastatingly evident than in
the case of Silvio Dante, the consigliere

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.560
of the DiMeo crime family and one of Tony's
most trusted friends and advisers.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.890
In the penultimate episode of the series,
"The Blue Comet," Lupertazzi boss Phil Leotardo

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.170
is on a quest for revenge and orders a series
of coordinated hits against the DiMeo crime

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.800
family for killing his brother.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.460
And sadly, that's when Silvio is introduced
to a couple of bullets.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.250
Silvio and Patsy Parisi are driving away from
the Bada Bing when two men open fire.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:37.789
Patsy starts shooting back and manages to
escape, but Silvio is hit several times, and

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:39.830
his wounds land him in an intensive care unit.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.329
In the series finale, "Made In America," it's
revealed that Silvio is comatose as a result

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:46.980
of his wounds, and the doctors don't expect
him to wake up.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.820
He spends the final episode of the series
on life support.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.940
Remember this it'll also be important later.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.780
One of the most debated and controversial
scenes in Sopranos history or maybe even,TV

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.660
history starts when Tony and his family meet
up for dinner.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:03.619
Tony plays Journey's "Don't Stop Believin'"
on the restaurant jukebox, and as the four

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:07.460
members of the Soprano family show up for
a meal, the tension becomes more and more

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:08.040
unbearable.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.660
We move from shady-looking guys in the restaurant
to Meadow struggling to park her car outside.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.550
Then, just as his daughter seems to be walking
in the restaurant, Tony looks up, and the

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:17.660
screen cuts to black.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:18.940
So … what happened?

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.700
Well, there are two basic interpretations,
so let's start with the most optimistic.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.280
Let's believe the cut to black is simply our
last moment with Tony Soprano, and we can

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.060
see that his future will be full of tension
and uncertainty.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.180
Yeah, the gang war was won, his kids seem
happy, and he's settling in for a quiet meal.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.260
But that guy at the counter is shady, anything
could've happened to Meadow out on the street,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.550
and Tony has to look up every time the bell
over the restaurant's door rings because he

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.100
has to be sure he's not being ambushed.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.840
Tony's alive, but he's never safe.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.600
If he survives the series, he's going to spend
the rest of his days fearing for his life,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.260
and that final scene was simply our connection
to him being closed off forever.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.980
Sure, maybe Tony Soprano made it out of the
show alive … but it's possible that Tony

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.680
had his brains blown out in that diner.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:01.300
It's entirely valid to believe that the screen
cuts to black because that's the moment where

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.060
Tony dies.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:06.160
The sudden cut is his point of view as a character,
with the sudden darkness showing that his

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.180
life has come to an end.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.840
There's actually some evidence backing up
this interpretation, as Tony's possible death

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.740
was foreshadowed earlier in the show.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.340
In the season six episode "Soprano Home Movies,"
Bobby Baccalieri and Tony are chatting about

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.830
the nature of death, when Bobby theorizes
that death is simple and quick.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.880
"Yeah, our line of work, it's always out there.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.800
You probably don't even hear it when it happens,
right?"

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.140
This little line strongly suggests that in
Tony's last moments, he didn't hear the bullet

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.640
that laid him low or even realize he was about
to die.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.560
Maybe the shady guy in the jacket went to
the bathroom to pick up a gun a la The Godfather,

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.740
came back out, and shot Tony in the head.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.340
We didn't see it because neither did Tony,
and it's possible that one of the greatest

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.660
characters in TV history was left for dead
in that New Jersey diner.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:48.620
So, which is it?

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.160
Is Tony Soprano alive or dead after that final
shot?

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.960
Series creator David Chase has always remained
famously cagey about explaining the scene,

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.520
and shortly after the last episode aired,
he insisted that "no one was trying to be

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.920
audacious" with the final moment.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.160
Chase has been asked several more times to
reflect on the ending in the years since the

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:08.280
series finale, and each time, he's remained
relatively ambiguous in his explanation.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.140
He's admitted that his original intention
was to kill Tony off at the end, but he changed

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.320
his mind and went with the sudden cut to black
instead.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.480
He explained, simply,

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.960
"I thought it was more interesting."

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.959
Maybe the best explanation for the scene from
the writers has come from Sopranos writer

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:21.800
Terence Winter:

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.599
"My interpretation was that, when you're Tony
Soprano, even going out for ice cream with

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.260
your family is fraught with paranoia.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:30.040
He's sown a life of murder, mayhem and treachery.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:34.120
And everybody who walks in, a guy in a Member's
Only jacket … this could be the guy, or

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.120
that could be the guy.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.439
You're always looking over your shoulder and
at some point, whether it happened that night

00:04:38.439 --> 00:04:42.300
or not, when you live that life, one day,
somebody's going to walk out of a men's room,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.300
and that's it for you."

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.460
When it comes to a story's ending, there are
two ways to take things: literally, and symbolically.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:53.110
So literally, whether you believe Bobby Baccalieri's
line about never hearing death coming or not,

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:56.100
we have a definitive answer for Tony Soprano:
he does not die.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.780
We know this, because he's still alive when
the screen cuts to black.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.580
Ending explained: Tony's alive.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:01.140
You're welcome.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:01.980
"Thank you.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:02.980
Thank you."

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.040
Symbolically, however, things are obviously
a lot more complicated.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.300
"How's it going?"

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:09.240
"You didn't get sick?"

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:10.400
"Nah."

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.900
But you don't need a talking fish to find
a potent symbol in The Sopranos.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.430
The events of the last two episodes actually
hint at a definitive explanation.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.400
We see in that episode that Dr. Melfi ends
her relationship with Tony, while Silvio is

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.140
shot and lingers in a coma neither alive nor
dead.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:28.400
The first episode of the series begins with
Tony meeting Dr. Melfi, while the second-to-last

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.020
episode shows that working relationship coming
to an end.

00:05:31.189 --> 00:05:36.020
So if the show is the story of Tony and Melfi's
sessions, the story concludes once those sessions

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:36.840
conclude too.

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:40.740
It doesn't seem like a coincidence that the
same episode features Silvio being shot, which

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.610
leads to him being rendered comatose, a state
in which he's technically alive...but for

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.200
all intents and purposes, he's not.

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.860
Silvio's coma mirrors Tony's end in the final
episode.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.280
He may be breathing, but he's seemingly in
a space between life and death already.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:56.499
So whether the final episode's cut to black
is a bullet in Tony's skull or a symbol that

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.500
his life's actual journey has come to an end
is pretty much irrelevant.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.880
As long as he pursues his worst impulses and
stays in his life of crime, he's as good as

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.480
dead every minute of the day.

